Two padding findings are in, and the memory-capacity check has started. Nothing needs you right now.

## Done this wake

**Padding: why left pads moved the slots.** Slots are the `<MEM>` vectors a page is compressed into. A 40-page probe found two separate effects:
- **One page alone, with pads:** the pads leak into the slots (cosine .935 in bf16, .953 in fp32, against 1.0 for unchanged). The installed transformers library only clears pads in Qwen3.5's linear-attention layers when a batch has more than one row. Forcing that clearing on brings it back to normal. No training run or eval uses this path: training batches always have at least 2 rows, and the eval adds no padding. It is a hidden bug, so I sent it to the main pisco chat (hand-off 2de131) to guard before anyone compresses one page at a time with padding.
- **Several pages, padded as in training:** in fp32 the slots don't change (1.0000). In bf16 they move by about the same amount as putting the page in a batch with any other page, with no padding at all (.9948 vs .9943). So this is bf16 rounding, not a padding effect.

**Padding: the eval wrapper check passed** (300 questions). My wrapper with padding off gives exactly the same answers as the shared eval script, 300 out of 300. With training-style padding, 67 of the 300 answers change. Whether that changes the judge score is what the two full runs now going will show.

**Memory-capacity check (can the model memorise 100 dense pages?):**
- Before its first run I fixed the page builder in two ways:
  - The training script silently moves 10% of rows into its own eval split, so on 100 pages it would have skipped 10. Each page is now repeated 10 times.
  - I added a ceiling check: each page's own text compared with the uncompressed model's transcription.
- I wrote the training launcher and the recall launcher. The builder test run passed in 1 minute: 100 + 100 pages of 674–760 tokens each.

**Campaign rules tightened:**
- The overfit training stops at a step count that fits the 2 GPU-hour limit, measured from the 20-step test.
- If its loss hasn't levelled off by then, the recall result counts only as a lower bound.
- In the photo repeat of the padding check, one-image questions are reported separately and don't decide the result.

## Running now
- **Padding vs judge accuracy:** two full runs of 1,500 questions, one per seed. Each judges padded and unpadded answers on the same node. They should finish around 23:00 and 23:20.
- **Memory check:** the full page-set build is running. Waiting on it are a 20-step training test and the starting model's recall on the 100 training pages and 100 held-out pages.

## Next
I'll wake when both padding runs and the training test have finished (3 hours at most). Then I apply the padding gate and launch the real overfit training, with its recall run chained after it.

Budget: 11 of 32 runs and about 0.8 of 30 GPU-hours used.

